# U-Boot config fragment for the PB2 CAN Carrier image.
# Verify against the pinned U-Boot tree (manifests/sources.lock:
# beagleboard/u-boot @ v2026.01-am62-pocketbeagle2, commit
# 7d2af1199011f2210a862097c78f1b940e5d2631) with scripts/kconfig/
# merge_config.sh before first build.
#
# Real defconfigs confirmed present in the pinned tree (two-stage K3
# boot: R5/SPL then A53/main U-Boot):
#   configs/am62_pocketbeagle2_r5_defconfig
#   configs/am62_pocketbeagle2_a53_defconfig
#
# Confirmed by actually configuring am62_pocketbeagle2_a53_defconfig
# locally: this board uses CONFIG_DM_SERIAL=y with driver-model serial,
# so console selection is DT-driven (via U-Boot's own copy of the board
# device tree, arch/arm/dts/k3-am62-pocketbeagle2-u-boot.dtsi + its
# synced dts/upstream base) -- CONFIG_CONS_INDEX does not apply to this
# target and has been removed from this fragment. CONFIG_BAUDRATE=115200
# and CONFIG_OF_LIBFDT_OVERLAY=y are already the defconfig default.
#
# STILL OPEN: scripts/assemble-image.sh currently uses BeagleBoard's own
# real, already-signed, unmodified bb-u-boot-pocketbeagle2 firmware
# package rather than a custom U-Boot build from this defconfig+fragment
# (see image-builder/README.md and RELEASE_NOTES.md item 1) -- that
# firmware's own SPL/U-Boot-stage console still defaults to MAIN_UART6,
# same as the Linux side before the carrier overlay/extlinux entry take
# over. Getting the very-earliest (pre-Linux, pre-extlinux) boot output
# onto UART0 too would need a real custom-signed U-Boot build (this
# defconfig + fragment + a stdout-path change in
# k3-am62-pocketbeagle2-u-boot.dtsi, then through TI's SYSFW/
# k3-image-gen signing) -- not done, and not blocking: the kernel/
# extlinux-stage console (the operationally relevant one) is already on
# UART0 per the "Boot flow" section below.
CONFIG_OF_BOARD_FIXUP=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot flow -- CONFIRMED by actually building and mounting a real image
# (scripts/assemble-image.sh, see image-builder/README.md for the full
# account). This whole section was corrected against ground truth after
# extracting BeagleBoard's own real, shipped extlinux template
# (bb-u-boot-pocketbeagle2's microsd-extlinux-w-swap.conf) instead of
# guessing from a disabled example comment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PB2 images boot via U-Boot distro_bootcmd + extlinux.conf (not the
# legacy uEnv.txt mechanism), read from the FAT boot partition's
# /extlinux/extlinux.conf. Real, confirmed syntax (lowercase, relative to
# the boot-partition root -- NOT the uppercase FDTOVERLAYS /boot/dtbs/
# path this fragment guessed earlier):
#
#   fdtdir /
#   fdtoverlays /overlays/k3-am62-pocketbeagle2-can-carrier.dtbo
#
# scripts/assemble-image.sh injects a new "PB2 CAN Carrier" label with
# this line (set as the default) after setup_sdcard.sh produces the
# image, alongside every stock label (kept intact for fallback/debug) --
# it does not modify image-builder's own templates.
#
# append kernel command line (work order §5.5), also injected by
# scripts/assemble-image.sh -- CONFIRMED on real hardware (see
# RELEASE_NOTES.md item 6):
#
#   console=ttyS7,115200n8 earlycon=ns16550a,mmio32,0x02860000
#
# - ttyS7 is UART0's real /dev/ttySN name (confirmed via `dmesg | grep
#   ttyS` on real hardware -- the earlier `ttyS0` guess was wrong; this
#   platform's 8250-omap driver does not call of_alias_get_id(), so
#   /dev/ttySN assignment is registration-order dependent, not DT-alias
#   dependent).
# - earlycon stays on UART6 (0x02860000), NOT UART0. UART0's peripheral
#   clock is never enabled by the stock, unmodified bb-u-boot-pocketbeagle2
#   firmware (which only drives MAIN_UART6), so pointing earlycon's raw
#   MMIO access at UART0 before Linux's clock driver has run hangs the
#   CPU outright (confirmed on real hardware -- total silence on both
#   UARTs, no USB enumeration). earlycon on UART6 is safe because U-Boot
#   already has that UART clocked and configured.
# - NO baud suffix on earlycon=. Stock BeagleBoard extlinux templates
#   also omit it. Per the kernel's earlycon docs, omitting the baud means
#   "inherit whatever U-Boot already configured" -- appending
#   `,115200n8` makes earlycon actively reprogram the UART against an
#   assumed reference clock, which produced garbled output (confirmed on
#   real hardware).
# - Only ONE console= argument (ttyS7), not two. Registering both ttyS7
#   and ttyS2 as simultaneous kernel consoles was confirmed on real
#   hardware to corrupt UART6's TTY output right at the ttyS7 driver
#   handoff (`printk: legacy console [ttyS7] disabled/enabled`), even
#   though the UART0 peripheral itself is fine in isolation (verified by
#   writing test strings directly to /dev/ttyS7 with `stty`/`printf`
#   while UART6 was still the sole console). Root cause not pinned down
#   further than "dual active kernel consoles on this 8250 setup
#   corrupts one of them during handoff" -- since the work order only
#   needs one working carrier console, the fix is simply not to enable
#   both. With ttyS7 alone, early boot messages (pre ~2.6s, before the
#   8250 driver takes over from the earlycon boot console) are only
#   visible on UART6's physical pin, not on UART0 -- a documented
#   limitation, same class as the SPL/U-Boot-stage limitation below.
# - The first `console=` argument wins for /dev/console; moot here since
#   there is only one.
